    name: { de: "Gorodoker Höhenzug", hu: "Haradoki-hátság", ro: "Podișul Gorodok", en: "Gorodok Upland"},
    image: "/poi-images/belarus-gorodok-upland-relief-v2.webp",
    coords: [30.0, 55.6],
    coords: [30.0, 55.6],
    description: {
      de: "Der Gorodoker Höhenzug liegt im Norden von Belarus und ist eine Fortsetzung des russischen Waldai-Hochlands. Er ist reich an Seen und bewaldeten Hügeln.",
      hu: "A Haradoki-hátság Észak-Fehéroroszországban fekszik, az orosz Valdaj-hátság folytatása. Tavakban és erdős dombokban gazdag terület.",
      ro: "Podișul Gorodok este situat în nordul Belarusului, fiind o continuare a Podișului Valdai din Rusia. Este bogat în lacuri și dealuri împădurite.",
      en: "The Gorodok Upland is located in northern Belarus and is a continuation of the Valdai Hills in Russia. It is rich in lakes and forested hills.",
    },
    facts: {
      de: ["Höchster Punkt 259 Meter", "Über 160 Seen in der Region", "Kühles, feuchtes Klima", "Hügelige Endmoräne"],
      hu: ["Legmagasabb pontja 259 méter", "Több mint 160 tó a régióban", "Hűvös, nedves éghajlat", "Dombos végmoréna"],
      ro: ["Altitudine maximă de 259 metri", "Peste 160 de lacuri în regiune", "Climat răcoros și umed", "Morenă terminală deluroasă"],
      en: ["Highest point is 259 meters", "Over 160 lakes in the region", "Cool and moist climate", "Hilly terminal moraine"],
    },
    imageHint: "Lakes and hills of Gorodok Upland",
    descriptionAdvanced: {
      de: "Der Gorodoker Höhenzug im äußersten Nordosten von Belarus ist eine geologische direkte Fortsetzung der bekannten russischen Waldai-Höhen. Dieses raue und kühle Hochland erreicht an seinem höchsten Punkt eine beachtliche Höhe von 259 Metern über dem Meeresspiegel. Die Landschaft ist ein Paradebeispiel für eine junge glaziale Topografie, geprägt von den steilen, unruhigen Endmoränen der letzten, der Waldai-Vergletscherung. Was den Gorodoker Höhenzug absolut einzigartig macht, ist sein unglaublicher Reichtum an Seen: Über 160 natürliche Gewässer schmiegen sich in die tiefen Gletschersenken zwischen den schroffen Hügeln und schaffen einen starken landschaftlichen Kontrast. Aufgrund seiner Höhenlage und der nordöstlichen Position weist die Region ein deutlich kühleres und feuchteres Mikroklima auf als der Rest des Landes. Dementsprechend ist das Gebiet schwer landwirtschaftlich nutzbar und stattdessen von dichten, fast taigaartigen Fichtenwäldern überzogen, die den steilen Hügeln und tiefblauen Seen einen wilden, unberührten Charakter verleihen.",
      hu: "A Fehéroroszország legészakkeletibb részén fekvő Haradoki-hátság földtanilag a jól ismert orosz Valdaj-hátság közvetlen folytatása. Ez a zord és hűvös hegyvidék a legmagasabb pontján eléri a tekintélyes 259 méteres tengerszint feletti magasságot. A táj a fiatal glaciális topográfia tankönyvbe illő példája, amelyet a legutóbbi, a Valdaj-eljegesedés meredek, tagolt végmorénái alakítottak ki. Ami a Haradoki-hátságot abszolút egyedülállóvá teszi, az a hihetetlen tó-gazdagsága: több mint 160 természetes víztest simul bele a meredek dombok közötti mély gleccserkatlanokba, éles tájképi kontrasztot teremtve. Tengerszint feletti magassága és északkeleti fekvése miatt a régió mikroklímája érezhetően hűvösebb és csapadékosabb, mint az ország többi részéé. Ennek megfelelően a terület mezőgazdasági művelésre alig alkalmas, ehelyett sűrű, szinte a tajgát idéző lucfenyvesek borítják, amelyek a meredek domboknak és a mélykék tavaknak vad, érintetlen karaktert kölcsönöznek.",
      ro: "Podișul Gorodok, situat în extremitatea nord-estică a Belarusului, este o continuare geologică directă a faimosului Podiș Valdai din Rusia. Acest ținut înalt, aspru și răcoros, atinge la cel mai înalt punct al său o altitudine remarcabilă de 259 de metri deasupra nivelului mării. Peisajul este un exemplu clasic de topografie glaciară tânără, fiind modelat de morenele terminale abrupte și neregulate ale ultimei glaciațiuni, glaciațiunea Valdai. Ceea ce face ca Podișul Gorodok să fie absolut unic este abundența sa incredibilă de lacuri: peste 160 de corpuri de apă naturale se cuibăresc în depresiunile glaciare adânci dintre dealurile abrupte, creând un contrast peisagistic puternic. Datorită altitudinii și poziției sale nord-estice, regiunea prezintă un microclimat semnificativ mai rece și mai umed decât restul țării. În consecință, zona este dificil de utilizat pentru agricultură și este în schimb acoperită de păduri dense de molid, asemănătoare taigalei, care conferă dealurilor abrupte și lacurilor albastre adânci un caracter sălbatic și neatins.",
      en: "The Gorodok Upland is a prominent glacial highland situated in the extreme northeastern corner of Belarus, functioning geographically as a direct extension of the Russian Valdai Hills. Reaching a maximum elevation of 259 meters, the area exhibits a decidedly rugged and deeply dissected relief, massively sculpted by the advances of the last Ice Age. The landscape is characterized by steep, asymmetrical kame-like hills, deep kettle hole depressions, and massive terminal moraine ridges. The impermeable glacial loams in the subsoil have facilitated the formation of over 160 lakes, which weave through the forested valleys like a glittering blue chain. Due to the heavy soils heavily interspersed with granite erratic boulders, coupled with a cooler, harsher macroclimate, agriculture plays a minor role here. Instead, the topography is dominated by dense, primordial taiga forests consisting of spruce, pine, and birch. This inaccessible ridge holds crucial hydrological significance as it forms the primary source area for numerous minor rivers that ultimately drain into the Western Dvina.",
//       en: "The Gorodok Upland in the extreme northeast of Belarus serves as a direct geological continuation of the well-known Russian Valdai Hills. This rugged and cool highland reaches a substantial elevation of 259 meters above sea level at its highest peak. The landscape is a prime example of young glacial topography, characterized by the steep, chaotic terminal moraines left behind by the last, or Valdai, glaciation. What makes the Gorodok Upland absolutely unique is its incredible abundance of lakes: over 160 natural bodies of water nestle into the deep glacial kettles between the abrupt hills, creating a stark and beautiful scenic contrast. Due to its elevation and extreme northeastern geographic position, the region experiences a significantly cooler and more humid microclimate than the rest of the country. Consequently, the terrain is highly unsuitable for large-scale agriculture and is instead cloaked in dense, almost taiga-like spruce forests, which give the steep hills and deep-blue lakes a wild, pristine character."
    },
